**`compareOpportunities`'s `outcomeSupportOf` tie-break criterion (`ranking.ts`)** — the JSDoc documents an 8-step chain with step 3 being "outcome support desc," but every visible test in `ranking.test.ts` only exercises the default `() => 0` (via the `noCorr`/no-arg call sites) or skips straight from verdict to corroboration. No test ever passes a non-trivial `outcomeSupportOf` to prove it sits between verdict rank and corroboration in priority. This is the same reinforcement-seam logic the memory notes flagged as recently shipped (`outcomeSupportFromCounts`), so it's a live code path with zero coverage of its ranking interaction.

```ts
describe("compareOpportunities tie-break: outcome support (step 3, verdict > outcome > corroboration)", () => {
  test("equal ice + verdict: higher outcome support wins over corroboration", () => {
    const a = mk({ id: "a" });
    const b = mk({ id: "b" });
    const outcomeOf = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "a" ? 2 : -1);
    const corr = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "b" ? 10 : 0); // b "wins" on corroboration alone
    expect(compareOpportunities(b, a, corr, outcomeOf)).toBeGreaterThan(0); // a still wins
  });

  test("outcome support does not override a higher verdict rank", () => {
    const shipped = mk({ id: "a", critic_review: critic("ship") });
    const revised = mk({ id: "b", critic_review: critic("revise") });
    const outcomeFavoringB = (o: RankableOpportunity) => (o.id === "b" ? 3 : -3);
    expect(compareOpportunities(revised, shipped, noCorr, outcomeFavoringB)).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  });
});
```

**`outcomeSupportFromCounts` (`ranking.ts`)** — pure function, exported, no visible test.

```ts
describe("outcomeSupportFromCounts", () => {
  test("validated minus missed", () => {
    expect(outcomeSupportFromCounts(2, 1)).toBe(1);
  });
  test("caps each side at 3", () => {
    expect(outcomeSupportFromCounts(10, 0)).toBe(3);
    expect(outcomeSupportFromCounts(0, 10)).toBe(-3);
  });
  test("clamps negative inputs to zero before capping", () => {
    expect(outcomeSupportFromCounts(-5, -5)).toBe(0);
  });
});
```

**`deriveDesignation`** — imported by `ranking.test.ts` but no visible assertions on it. Needs coverage for each `Designation` value ("best bet", "needs validation", "quick win", "heavy lift", "watch this week", `null`).

```ts
describe("deriveDesignation", () => {
  test("returns 'best bet' for the top-ranked opportunity", () => { /* ... */ });
  test("returns null when no designation criteria match", () => { /* ... */ });
  // one case per remaining Designation value, each pinned to the specific
  // ice/confidence/ease combination that earns it
});
```

**Signal-body humanization helpers in `format.ts`** — `signalPreview`, `signalCleanBody`, `signalHasRaw` are all imported in `format.test.ts`'s import list, but the visible test body never exercises them (only `readableFromJson`'s private caller path would prove them). Given the file's own comment calls out "an ingested connector payload can arrive as raw JSON... markdown with embedded image and link syntax, or long bare URLs" as the exact motivating cases, these need direct tests:

```ts
describe("signalCleanBody / signalPreview / signalHasRaw", () => {
  test("strips markdown image syntax from a body", () => {
    expect(signalCleanBody("Look ![alt](http://x/img.png) at this")).not.toContain("![");
  });
  test("drops bare long URLs so column width never bleeds", () => {
    expect(signalCleanBody("See https://example.com/very/long/path?x=1")).not.toContain("https://");
  });
  test("pulls a readable gist out of a raw JSON payload (title/description keys)", () => {
    const raw = JSON.stringify({ title: "Bug in checkout", description: "Users can't pay" });
    expect(signalPreview(raw)).toContain("Bug in checkout");
  });
  test("falls back to nested readable value when no title/description keys exist", () => {
    const raw = JSON.stringify({ comment: { text: "nested note" } });
    expect(signalPreview(raw)).toContain("nested note");
  });
  test("bounded recursion never spins on a deeply nested or hostile payload", () => {
    let deep: unknown = "leaf";
    for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) deep = { nested: deep };
    expect(() => signalPreview(JSON.stringify(deep))).not.toThrow();
  });
  test("signalHasRaw is true only when the body differs from its cleaned preview", () => {
    expect(signalHasRaw('{"title":"x"}')).toBe(true);
    expect(signalHasRaw("plain text, nothing to clean")).toBe(false);
  });
});
```

**`FigmaEmbed.renderHTML` and `addCommands`/`setFigmaEmbed`** — only `parseHTML` and `addAttributes` are tested. The actual HTML/iframe output (the security-relevant part, since `toEmbedUrl`'s output lands directly in an `iframe src`) is untested at the node level.

```ts
describe("FigmaEmbed.config.renderHTML()", () => {
  test("wraps a figma.com src through toEmbedUrl before setting iframe src", () => {
    const out = FigmaEmbed.config.renderHTML({ HTMLAttributes: { src: "https://www.figma.com/file/abc" } });
    const iframe = out[2];
    expect(iframe[1].src).toContain("figma.com/embed");
  });
  test("passes a non-figma src through unchanged", () => {
    const out = FigmaEmbed.config.renderHTML({ HTMLAttributes: { src: "https://example.com/deck.pdf" } });
    expect(out[2][1].src).toBe("https://example.com/deck.pdf");
  });
  test("iframe always carries sandbox=allow-same-origin (no allow-scripts)", () => {
    const out = FigmaEmbed.config.renderHTML({ HTMLAttributes: { src: "" } });
    expect(out[2][1].sandbox).toBe("allow-same-origin");
  });
});

describe("FigmaEmbed.addCommands().setFigmaEmbed", () => {
  test("inserts a figmaEmbed node with the given src", () => {
    const insertContent = mock((c: unknown) => true);
    const cmd = FigmaEmbed.config.addCommands().setFigmaEmbed({ src: "https://figma.com/file/x" });
    cmd({ commands: { insertContent } });
    expect(insertContent).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ type: "figmaEmbed", attrs: { src: "https://figma.com/file/x" } });
  });
});
```

**`ResearchActivityLine` / `ResearchSummaryRow` components** — imported into `ResearchActivity.test.ts` but only the pure helpers (`parseResearchStatus`, `summarySegments`) are visibly tested; no render-level test of either component (e.g., via `@testing-library/react`).

**`barInsight`** — the visible tests cover single-point, flat, up, down, mixed-sign endpoints, and all-negative, but not a series whose *peak* is neither the first nor last point (the function explicitly names "peak" in its output, so a mid-series spike/trough is the case most likely to break the peak-finding logic):

```ts
test("names a mid-series peak, not just the endpoints", () => {
  const data = d([2, 3, 9, 4]);
  const s = barInsight(data, fmt);
  expect(s).toContain("peak 9 on w3");
});
```

**`traceRef`** — boundary at exactly 6 alphanumerics vs. fewer/more is implicitly covered by "a1b2c3d4..." (>6) and "a-1" (<6), but the exact-6 boundary itself isn't pinned:

```ts
test("returns exactly 6 chars unchanged in length when the id has exactly 6 alphanumerics", () => {
  expect(traceRef("ab-cd-ef")).toBe("ABCDEF");
});
```

**`withTimeout`** — the visible excerpt only shows the resolve-before-deadline case; the reject/timeout path itself (the actual reason the helper exists, per its own comment about h3-swallowed 500s) isn't shown:

```ts
test("rejects with a friendly message when the promise never settles before the deadline", async () => {
  const hung = new Promise(() => {}); // never resolves
  await expect(withTimeout(hung, 50)).rejects.toThrow("The server took too long to answer. Retry in a moment.");
});
test("clears its internal timer on success so no open handle leaks into the test runner", async () => {
  const spy = spyOn(global, "clearTimeout");
  await withTimeout(Promise.resolve("ok"), 1000);
  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

**`resolveKindColors`** — the visible test only checks the DOM/getComputedStyle path via a mocked `window`; the SSR fallback (`typeof window === "undefined"`) branch isn't exercised:

```ts
test("falls back to literal color values when window is undefined (SSR)", () => {
  const originalWindow = globalThis.window;
  // @ts-expect-error simulate SSR
  delete globalThis.window;
  const colors = resolveKindColors({} as HTMLElement);
  expect(colors.get("signal")).toBe("#e5bddf"); // the literal fallback, not a resolved token
  globalThis.window = originalWindow;
});
```

- **Duplicate/near-duplicate test coverage across `Sketch.test.ts`, `__tests__/sketch-helpers.test.ts`, and `__tests__/sketch-insight.test.ts`** all re-test `mulberry32`, `seedOf`, `sketchPath`, `capFirst`, and `barInsight` with overlapping assertions. This isn't a gap so much as drift risk: three files can silently diverge on what "correct" means for the same function. Recommend consolidating into one canonical test file per source module (keep `sketch-insight.test.ts`'s honest-takeaway framing since it documents intent best, fold the others in) rather than adding more tests here.
- **`SketchLine`, `SketchBar`, `SketchBarChart` components themselves** — every visible test targets the pure helper functions (`sketchPath`, `barInsight`, etc.) that back these charts, but none render the actual React components. Given the recent commit "SketchBarChart negative values," a render-level test would catch layout/scaling regressions the pure-function tests can't see:

```ts
import { render } from "@testing-library/react";
test("SketchBarChart renders bars below the baseline for negative values", () => {
  const { container } = render(<SketchBarChart data={[{ label: "a", value: -50 }, { label: "b", value: 50 }]} />);
  // assert on the rendered <path>/<rect> y-position or a baseline-crossing class
});
```

- **`graph-visual.test.ts` vs. `__tests__/graph-visual.test.ts`** both target `src/components/knowledge/graph-visual.ts` but assert different `KIND_VISUAL` contents (e.g. one expects `kindVisual("decision")` to resolve to `--ember-soft`/"Decision", which isn't present in the truncated `KIND_VISUAL` map shown for the other test's source read). Worth a scenario that pins the *complete* `KIND_VISUAL` map in one place (a snapshot test) so the two suites can't silently test against two different mental models of the same map:

```ts
test("KIND_VISUAL has exactly the kinds both graph-visual test suites assume", () => {
  expect(Object.keys(KIND_VISUAL).sort()).toEqual(
    ["decision", "design_memory", "meeting", "mission", "opportunity", "prd", "roadmap_item", "signal", "task", "theme"].sort(),
  );
});
```

- **`readableFromJson` / `signalPreview` on malformed JSON input** — `signalPreview`/`signalCleanBody` presumably call `JSON.parse` internally on a raw signal body; there's no visible test for a body that *isn't* valid JSON at all (the common real-world case for a plain-text signal), confirming it degrades to plain-text cleaning rather than throwing:

```ts
test("signalPreview does not throw on non-JSON body, just cleans it as plain text", () => {
  expect(() => signalPreview("plain text signal, not json")).not.toThrow();
});
```

- **`toEmbedUrl` with a `URL`-parseable but attribute-injection payload** — the security suite covers hostname spoofing thoroughly, but not a src containing characters that could break out of the `iframe src="..."` attribute if `encodeURIComponent` were ever removed (a regression-guard test, not a current bug):

```ts
test("regression guard: encodes quotes/angle-brackets so the result is never attribute-breaking raw", () => {
  const src = 'https://www.figma.com/file/abc?x="><script>alert(1)</script>';
  const result = toEmbedUrl(src);
  expect(result).not.toContain('"><script>');
});
```

- **`parseResearchStatus` — malformed `phase`/`label` combinations beyond the ones shown** — extra unexpected keys on an otherwise-valid object (should still parse, ignoring extras) and a `label` that's a non-empty string but `phase` is a valid enum value with mixed types elsewhere on the object:

```ts
test("ignores unrecognized extra keys on an otherwise-valid status object", () => {
  const result = parseResearchStatus({ phase: "plan", label: "Planning...", extra: 123 });
  expect(result).not.toBeNull();
});
```

- **Discover ranking pipeline end-to-end** — `verdictFor` (format.ts) feeds `verdictRankOf`/`compareOpportunities` (ranking.ts), which feeds `rankOpportunities`. Each is unit-tested in isolation, but there's no test that a `CriticReview`-shaped fixture flows correctly from raw opportunity row through to a `RankedOpportunity` with the right `designation`, `rationale`, and `outcomeSupport` populated together — the exact integration point where a field-name mismatch between the two modules would slip through unit tests.

```ts
test("a critic-shipped, high-corroboration opportunity ranks #1 with 'best bet' and a rationale mentioning the Critic", () => {
  const opps = [mk({ id: "a", critic_review: critic("ship") }), mk({ id: "b" })];
  const [top] = rankOpportunities(opps, (o) => (o.id === "a" ? 5 : 0));
  expect(top.opp.id).toBe("a");
  expect(top.isBestBet).toBe(true);
  expect(top.rationale.toLowerCase()).toContain("critic");
});
```

- **FigmaEmbed inside an actual TipTap editor instance** — `parseHTML`/`renderHTML`/`addCommands` are each unit-tested against the node config object directly, but never through a real `Editor` instance calling `setFigmaEmbed` and round-tripping to/from HTML — the level at which a TipTap API change (e.g. `mergeAttributes` behavior) would actually break.

- **`ResearchActivity` phase transitions over time** — `parseResearchStatus` validates one snapshot at a time; there's no test simulating a sequence of phases (`plan` → `search` → `read` → `workspace` → `synthesize`) driving `ResearchActivityLine` re-renders, which is the actual user-visible behavior during a live research run.

- **Signal ingestion → `signalCleanBody`/`signalPreview` → Discover feed render** — the humanization helpers are pure and (per gap #1) untested even in isolation; once added, a follow-up integration test feeding a raw connector payload (e.g. a Linear MCP issue blob, called out explicitly in the source comment as "the canonical offender") through to the rendered feed row would confirm the whole pipeline, not just the pure function.

